            "learn": {
                "paragraphs": [
                    "Application of integrals is primarily used to find the area under curves. If a curve \\( y = f(x) \\) is above the x-axis, the area bounded by the curve, the x-axis, and the vertical lines \\( x = a \\) and \\( x = b \\) is the definite integral.",
                    "**Case 1: Area with x-axis**",
                    "\\( Area = \\int_a^b |y| dx = \\int_a^b |f(x)| dx \\)",
                    "**Case 2: Area with y-axis**",
                    "\\( Area = \\int_c^d |x| dy = \\int_c^d |g(y)| dy \\)",
                    "**Important:** If the area is below the x-axis, the integral will be negative. We take the absolute value (magnitude) since area is always positive."
                ],
                "formulas": [
                    {
                        "rule": "Area Formula (x-axis)",
                        "formula": "A = \\int_a^b y dx",
                        "example": "Linear boundaries x=a, x=b."
